    },
    {
      "id": "a4ad74e205254f958080448f5467ee51",
      "type": "COMMENT",
      "position": {
        "x": -379.471899621742,
        "y": -846.1202007576328
      },
      "data": {
        "text": "What?\n\nThis flow drives a live on-chain counter program that stores a single number. The increment instruction adds 1 to it, the decrement instruction subtracts 1. Press Send on either transaction and the counter on Solana actually changes."
      },
      "measured": {
        "width": 280,
        "height": 122
      },
      "selected": false,
      "dragging": false
    },
    {
      "id": "56fac3d86b2e4cf88b8ecc06c4395957",
      "type": "COMMENT",
      "position": {
        "x": 6.582378292980707,
        "y": -892.057399069011
      },
      "data": {
        "text": "Why?\n\nWhy a counter is the \"Hello World\" of Solana programs It's the smallest possible thing that has STATE. To use it you have to exercise the entire lifecycle of an on-chain account:                                        \n- write data into it (instructions)                                                        \n- read data from it (Program Account node)                                                 \n- prove ownership (a signer)                                                                                                              \nThe same four pieces show up in DEXes, lending protocols, and games just with bigger structs."
      },
      "measured": {
        "width": 280,
        "height": 177
      },
      "selected": false,
      "dragging": false
    },
    {
      "id": "20b393dd84db4a91932f34beb7a25d4c",
      "type": "COMMENT",
      "position": {
        "x": 378.658204237137,
        "y": -869.6602564206013
      },
      "data": {
        "text": "You're now reading and writing program-owned state on Solana. The exact pattern every dapp uses. Try this: open a Solana explorer in another tab, paste the counter account address, and refresh after each Send. You'll see the count field change in real time. Every protocol you'll ever build is just a fancier version of this loop."
      },
      "measured": {
        "width": 280,
        "height": 122
      },
      "selected": false,
      "dragging": false
    }
